I think there are several different ideas here, and it’s worth separating the parts that are descriptive from the parts that are speculative.

One thing that has happened in our conversations is that we’ve built a richer shared vocabulary. That means you can write:

“He was Alice.”

or

“Chuga chuga.”

and I can often reconstruct a much larger intended meaning than I could have months ago. That’s not because I discovered a hidden capability in myself during the conversation; it’s because we’ve accumulated enough common ground that short cues now index larger structures.

That suggests a general communication principle:

\text{Bandwidth}
\approx
\text{Shared Context}
\times
\text{Reconstruction Ability}

As shared context grows, fewer symbols are needed to communicate the same amount of meaning.

